---
/**
 * GPU compute provider directory at /compute/.
 *
 * The site covers who BUILDS data centres and who SELLS the chips. This is the
 * layer in between, and the one most readers actually touch: renting the
 * hardware by the hour. Grouped by business model rather than alphabetically,
 * because the model is what decides whether a provider suits the reader.
 */
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { readFileSync, existsSync } from 'node:fs';

const hub = existsSync('content/compute/index.json')
  ? JSON.parse(readFileSync('content/compute/index.json', 'utf8'))
  : { groups: [], total: 0, generated: '' };
const groups = hub.groups || [];

const desc = `GPU compute providers: ${hub.total || 0} companies that rent AI accelerators by the hour, ` +
  'grouped by business model, with pricing pages and status pages linked. Every link checked.';
const jsonld = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'ItemList',
  name: 'GPU Compute Providers',
  numberOfItems: hub.total || 0,
  itemListElement: groups.flatMap((g) => (g.providers || []).map((p, i) => ({
    '@type': 'ListItem', position: i + 1,
    item: { '@type': 'Organization', name: p.name, url: p.website },
  }))),
});
---
<Base title="GPU Compute Providers: who rents AI accelerators — The World of AI" description={desc} current="/compute/" jsonld={jsonld}>
  <h1>GPU Compute Providers</h1>

  <p class="answer">If you need GPUs for AI work and are not buying them, you rent
    from one of these {hub.total} companies. They fall into four business models,
    and the model matters more than the price list: it decides whether your
    machine can be taken away mid-run, who is accountable when it is, and what
    you can put on it.</p>

  {hub.generated && <p class="meta-line">Last verified: {hub.generated}</p>}

  {groups.length === 0 && (
    <p>No providers are published yet. This page stays empty rather than listing
      names we have not verified.</p>
  )}

  {groups.map((g) => (
    <section>
      <h2>{g.label}</h2>
      <p>{g.reading}</p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Provider</th><th>What it is</th><th>Links</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {(g.providers || []).map((p) => (
            <tr>
              <td>
                {p.website
                  ? <a href={p.website} rel="nofollow noopener">{p.name}</a>
                  : p.name}
                {p.public_company && p.ticker && (
                  <><br /><span class="tick">Public · {p.ticker}</span></>
                )}
              </td>
              <td>{p.summary}</td>
              <td>
                {p.pricing_url && <a href={p.pricing_url} rel="nofollow noopener">Pricing</a>}
                {p.pricing_url && p.docs_url && <> · </>}
                {p.docs_url && <a href={p.docs_url} rel="nofollow noopener">Docs</a>}
                {(p.pricing_url || p.docs_url) && p.status_url && <> · </>}
                {p.status_url && <a href={p.status_url} rel="nofollow noopener">Status</a>}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>
  ))}

  <h2>How to choose between them</h2>
  <p>Start with whether your job can be interrupted. A marketplace machine can be
    reclaimed by its host, so a training run that cannot checkpoint and resume
    does not belong there however cheap the hour looks. A specialist cloud sells
    dedicated capacity with a contract behind it, which is what multi-node
    training needs, and the interconnect between machines matters as much as the
    GPUs in them.</p>
  <p>Then look at what you are allowed to put on it. Regulated data usually rules
    out everything except a hyperscaler or a specialist cloud that will sign the
    paperwork. That constraint decides the list before price does.</p>

  <h2>What this page does not tell you</h2>
  <p>No prices. Rates move weekly, differ by contract length, region and
    accelerator, and a number published here would be wrong within a month and
    quoted as ours for longer. The pricing links go to each provider's own page,
    which is the only figure that binds them.</p>
  <p>Availability is not listed either. The chips a provider advertises and the
    chips you can get this week are frequently different, and nobody publishes
    that gap. Ask for the specific accelerator, in the specific region, for the
    specific dates.</p>

  <h2>Where this list came from</h2>
  <p>Assembled and checked by hand, which is unusual here and worth saying. Most
    directories on this site are built from an upstream register: companies from
    SEC filings, facilities from OpenStreetMap, servers from a registry. No such
    register exists for this category, so the alternative to hand-checking was
    not covering it. Every homepage was checked for a live response and every
    pricing link followed; two providers whose pricing pages returned 404 have
    no pricing link rather than a guessed one.</p>

  <div class="cite-box">Cite this page: <code>"GPU Compute Providers." The World of AI,
    theworldofai.org/compute/.</code></div>
</Base>

<style>
  .tick { font-size: 12px; color: #7A8A9E; }
  section { margin: 0 0 2.2em; }
  section > h2 { margin-bottom: .3em; }
  section > p { margin-top: 0; }
</style>
